<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ypres</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ieper</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ypres (Ieper) is a city in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was one of the three great cloth towns of the County of Flanders, with a vast Cloth Hall, and one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-members-of-flanders">Four Members of Flanders</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it lay at the centre of a salient that saw some of the war's heaviest fighting; the town was destroyed and rebuilt, and the Menin Gate commemorates the missing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ypres.jpg" alt="Ypres" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marc Ryckaert · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ieper_Grote_Markt_R04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ypres, officially Ieper in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, is a city and municipality in the province of West Flanders, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. The municipality includes villages such as Boezinge, Brielen, Zillebeke and Vlamertinge and has about 34,900 inhabitants. The town is known to have been raided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in the first century BCE, is first mentioned by name in 1066, and is probably named after the river Ieperlee, on whose banks it was founded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Middle Ages Ypres was a prosperous Flemish city with 40,000 people in 1200, known for its linen trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. As the third largest city of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, it played an important part in the textile industry, and its cloth reached Novgorod in the early 12th century. The Cloth Hall was built in the 13th century. A fire in 1241 ruined much of the old city, and during the Norwich Crusade of Bishop Henry le Despenser it was besieged from May to August 1383 until French relief forces arrived. It became the seat of a new diocese in 1561.</p><p>Forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> took it on 25 March 1678, and Vauban built the fortifications still visible. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1713, and the French captured it again in 1794.</p><p>In the First World War Ypres stood in the path of the German sweep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from the north. The Germans surrounded it on three sides and bombarded it for much of the war, while British, French and allied troops made costly advances from the Ypres Salient. In the Second Battle of Ypres, which began on 22 April 1915, the Germans used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a> for the first time on a large scale, chlorine, and mustard gas was first used near the town in autumn 1917. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-passchendaele">Third Battle of Ypres</a>, or Passchendaele, from 31 July to 10 November 1917, brought nearly half a million casualties to all sides for only a few miles gained. The town was all but obliterated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the war Ypres was rebuilt with German reparations, its main square, Cloth Hall and town hall as close to the originals as possible. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> decided in September 1920 that the Menin Gate would be a memorial, and it was unveiled on 24 July 1927; it names Commonwealth soldiers who fell in the Salient and have no known grave. Since 1929 the Last Post has been sounded there each evening at eight o'clock, apart from the German occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, when it was held at Brookwood Military Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The Cloth Hall, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1999, houses the In Flanders Fields Museum, and the Cat Parade is held every three years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ypres” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Land reclaimed from the sea and the coastal marshes supported large flocks of sheep, and from the eleventh century the towns of Ghent, Bruges, <b>Ypres</b>, Arras, Douai and Lille specialised in high-quality woollen cloth, increasingly made from imported English wool.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>German troops released chlorine from cylinders against French and Algerian positions near <b>Ypres</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-22">22 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
